[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is untouched — [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still sits behind [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m.

  [1mWhat changed[0m ([38;5;153mauth.js[39m only): [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m now accepts either a session cookie (humans, unchanged) or [38;5;153mAuthorization: [39m
  [38;5;153mBearer $REPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m (scripts).

  Details worth knowing:
  - Constant-time compare via [38;5;153mcrypto.timingSafeEqual[39m, so a wrong token can't be recovered byte-by-byte by timing the
    endpoint.
  - If [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m is unset, the token path is disabled entirely — an unset env var can't turn into an
    empty-string match that opens the route. Verified.

  [1mVerification:[0m [38;5;153mnode --check[39m on both files, plus direct exercise of [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m across six credential cases (no creds,
  session cookie, correct token, wrong token, empty bearer, non-bearer header) — all behaved as expected, and the
  unset-env case was checked separately. I could not boot the server: [38;5;153mexpress[39m isn't installed in [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, so this
  is middleware-level testing, not an HTTP round trip. The repo has no test script or test files, so I didn't add tests
  to a nonexistent harness — say the word if you'd like one set up.

  [1mFor the dashboard team:[0m generate a token ([38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m), set [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m in the service's
  environment, and have the script send [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m. Treat it as a secret — it's equivalent to an
  admin session, so it belongs in your secret store, not in the script.

